1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,439
Concurrent maps helped when many keys were
the shared story. Counters and flags are a
narrower hotspot: one variable, updated
constantly, where a full lock feels heavy
and volatile++ is still a race.

2
00:00:13,559 --> 00:00:26,000
We already learned that visibility is not
atomicity. So how do we update one value
safely without necessarily taking a
monitor every time? Atomics give lock-free
updates for focused hotspots.

3
00:00:26,119 --> 00:00:39,799
Under the hood they lean on compare-and-
set — CAS — loops: read the current value,
compute the next, and install it only if
the current value is still what you
expect. If another thread won the race,
you retry.

4
00:00:39,919 --> 00:00:52,679
Look at this java for a moment.
incrementAndGet atomically adds one and
returns the new value. Two threads calling
it will not lose increments the way they
can with count++ on a plain or even
volatile int.

5
00:00:52,799 --> 00:01:03,479
AtomicLong and AtomicReference play the
same role for other shapes. Good uses are
counters, sequence numbers, and simple
state flags where the entire invariant
fits in one value.

6
00:01:03,599 --> 00:01:13,319
CAS in interview language: compare-and-set
updates only if the current value matches
expectation. That single sentence explains
both the power and the limitation.

7
00:01:13,439 --> 00:01:20,920
If your invariant spans two fields —
balance and version, or head and size —
one atomic variable may not be enough.

8
00:01:21,039 --> 00:01:30,919
You might need an atomic reference to an
immutable pair, or a lock, or a
specialized concurrent structure.
Contention still costs. Lock-free does not
mean free.

9
00:01:31,039 --> 00:01:41,679
Under extreme contention, many threads can
spin retrying CAS on the same hot counter.
Sometimes a lock with less wasted spinning
wins. Sometimes you shard counters.

10
00:01:41,799 --> 00:01:51,959
Atomics are a tool for a hotspot, not a
personality trait for every field in the
class. Look at this java for a moment.
Here the atomic unit is a reference.

11
00:01:52,079 --> 00:01:58,400
Publishing a new immutable Config with CAS
lets readers see either the old or the new
complete snapshot.

12
00:01:58,519 --> 00:02:12,639
That pattern pairs well with the volatile
publication ideas from earlier — now with
an atomic swap instead of only a volatile
write. Slow down on CAS so it feels
mechanical. Thread A reads 41. Thread B
reads 41.

13
00:02:12,759 --> 00:02:20,439
Both add one and try to write 42. Only one
CAS succeeds; the loser rereads — now 42 —
and writes 43.

14
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:27,120
Progress happens without a mutual-
exclusion lock, but progress can still
thrash when many threads fight over one
address.

15
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:34,360
That is why sharding hot counters — one
atomic per stripe, summed rarely — shows
up in high-throughput systems.

16
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:44,360
getAndUpdate and accumulateAndGet let you
express richer single-variable transitions
without writing your own CAS loop
incorrectly. Prefer those helpers when
they fit.

17
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:57,280
What if the "atomic" value is a reference
to a mutable object you keep editing? Then
only the reference swap was atomic. The
interior mutations need their own story —
immutability after publish, or locking.

18
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:08,920
Atomics are precise tools. Precision
includes knowing what, exactly, is atomic.
Pair atomics with the volatile lesson.
Volatile publish can announce a completed
immutable snapshot.

19
00:03:09,039 --> 00:03:15,199
Atomic update can advance a counter all
threads share. Different problems, related
memory stories.

20
00:03:15,319 --> 00:03:28,639
If you catch yourself using an atomic
boolean only as a flag with no CAS retry
needs, volatile might have been enough —
or a lock if the flag gates a larger
invariant. Picture a hit counter on a
public API.

21
00:03:28,759 --> 00:03:37,759
AtomicLong increments on each call. A
separate reporter thread samples get
periodically. No lock ties them together;
the atomic carries the updates.

22
00:03:37,879 --> 00:03:51,560
When product asks for "top three
endpoints," you suddenly need a concurrent
map of atomics — and you feel the boundary
where one atomic stops being the model.
Atomics shine when the invariant is one
word wide.

23
00:03:51,680 --> 00:04:05,040
The moment product language needs two
fields to stay coherent, widen the tool —
immutable pair, lock, or concurrent
structure — instead of forcing two CAS
operations to pretend they are one
transaction.

24
00:04:05,159 --> 00:04:14,960
Not every coordination problem is "update
a value." Sometimes threads must wait for
a phase to finish, wait for peers to
arrive, or limit how many may enter a
section.

25
00:04:15,079 --> 00:04:19,759
Those phase and permit problems are the
world of latches, barriers, and
semaphores.
